; `@ =price` fixates the lot its own cost creates, and no other holding
; of it, so a fixated purchase, `10 AAPL @ =$5`, leaves the floating
; purchases at the same price on the same day, before and after it, in
; the unfixated lot {$5} [2020/01/01].  The Fixated account shows the
; fixation itself still applies: its lot is {=$5}, and -V holds it at $5
; while the others follow the later $7 price.
;
; All three costs describe the same lot, {$5} [2020/01/01], and the
; fixated purchase takes the fixated lot {=$5} [2020/01/01] in its place.
; Ledger used to mark that shared lot itself as fixated, so all three
; purchases showed as one 30 AAPL {=$5} lot and -V valued every
; share at $5.

P 2020/03/01 AAPL $7

2020/01/01 Buy floating
    Assets:Broker:First      10 AAPL @ $5
    Assets:Cash

2020/01/01 Buy fixated
    Assets:Broker:Fixated    10 AAPL @ =$5
    Assets:Cash

2020/01/01 Buy floating
    Assets:Broker:Last       10 AAPL @ $5
    Assets:Cash

test bal --lots Assets:Broker
 20 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01]
10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]  Assets:Broker
10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01]    First
10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]    Fixated
10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01]    Last
--------------------
 20 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01]
10 AAPL {=$5} [2020/01/01]
end test

test bal -V Assets:Broker
                $190  Assets:Broker
                 $70    First
                 $50    Fixated
                 $70    Last
--------------------
                $190
end test
